<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Ohrid</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> is one of the oldest and deepest lakes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. Greek writers placed the Illyrian Enchelei, the eel people, near its shores. Later the town of Ohrid on its north-eastern shore became a centre of Slavic Christian learning under Saint Clement and the seat of an archbishopric, and the region is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-ohrid.jpg" alt="Lake Ohrid" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA/METI/AIST/Japan Space Systems, and U.S./Japan ASTER Science Team · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Ohrid,_Macedonia-Albania_viewed_from_a_NASA_satellite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> lies in a tectonic basin on the mountainous border between the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. It is the deepest lake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, with a maximum depth of 288 metres, and covers 358 square kilometres. It is 30.4 kilometres long and 14.8 kilometres wide; 64 per cent of the shore and 69 per cent of the surface lie in North Macedonia and the rest in Albania. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-prespa">Lake Prespa</a> it belongs to a chain of grabens known as the Dessaret group. The oldest lake sediments are Pliocene, possibly 3 to 5 million years old. Ohrid survives through its depth, continuing subsidence and inflows from Lake Prespa that carry little sediment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The prehistoric pile-dwelling near Lin, at Pogradec in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, is the oldest of its kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, according to specialists at the University of Bern. The settlement is over 8,500 years old, and it developed agriculture 8,000 to 9,000 years ago, playing a part in spreading farming across Europe. It predates similar Swiss sites by more than 2,000 years. Archaeologists have excavated the remains.</p><p>The lake lay close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>, a main highway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> on the Albanian coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Religious monuments such as the Black Madonna south-west of Struga stand beside the traces of later wars, such as the fortifications of the Macedonian Front across Mount Galičica. The strongest earthquake measured in the area struck on 18 February 1911, with a magnitude of 6.6, and destroyed houses without loss of life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The towns on the shore are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> and Struga in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and Pogradec in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. The Macedonian side of the lake was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979, and in 1980 the site was extended to the cultural and historic area of Ohrid, making it a mixed natural and cultural site. The Albanian side was added in 2019. The lake has more than 200 endemic species, including the Ohrid trout complex, and almost 80 per cent of its mollusc species are endemic. The Macedonian and Albanian parts are each an Important Bird Area, and the lakeshore wetlands shelter hundreds of thousands of wintering water birds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Ohrid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek writers from Hecataeus and Herodotus onward place the Enchelei, the 'eel people', near <b>Lake Ohrid</b> and the Drin valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/enchelei">Enchelei</a></li></ul></section>
